---
import LabsLayout from "../../layouts/labs.astro";
---

<LabsLayout title="Load and test packages — Querial Labs" active="run-through">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">Labs</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Load and test</h1>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 max-w-2xl">
                Same Workspace path for every scenario. Import a zip, resolve connections,
                publish, create a Development deployment, trigger a run, then open Run
                details and Run Graph. Roles below match what the product actually allows.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground [&>ol]:text-muted-foreground">
            <h2>0. Project and flags</h2>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
                <li>In the sidebar footer, select the team / project that should own the lab. Import never copies into another project.</li>
                <li>Confirm you can see <strong class="text-foreground">Pipelines</strong> (Designer or operator) and, for import, the <strong class="text-foreground">Import package</strong> button (CanOperate only).</li>
                <li>For B, C-PostgreSQL, D-PostgreSQL, and E: Artifacts and DuckDB extension flags must be on. Without them, extract/staged/artifact-sql handlers will not run.</li>
                <li>For F: Artifacts and DAG execution must be on. Trigger with a multipart file. Do not create a schedule.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>1. Import package</h2>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">DevOps.</strong> Pipelines list →
                <strong class="text-foreground">Import package</strong>. The modal is two steps.
            </p>
            <h3 class="text-foreground text-lg font-semibold">Step 1 — Upload</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Choose the scenario zip (or <code class="text-foreground">aw-lab-all.zip</code>). Max 64&nbsp;MB.</li>
                <li>Optional: <strong class="text-foreground">Overwrite existing non-empty drafts</strong> if you already imported that pipeline code.</li>
                <li>Optional: <strong class="text-foreground">Replace README when package README is missing</strong>.</li>
                <li>Click <strong class="text-foreground">Start import</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="text-foreground text-lg font-semibold">Step 2 — Resolve connections</h3>
            <p>
                Catalog stubs have no endpoints. For each package name
                (<code class="text-foreground">aw-source</code>,
                <code class="text-foreground">aw-sql-dest</code>,
                <code class="text-foreground">aw-pg-dest</code> as required):
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Use existing</strong> — map to a logical connection already in this project (same provider).</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Create connection</strong> — new logical name; optionally <strong class="text-foreground">Save development endpoint now</strong> and fill host, catalog, credentials, timeouts in the nested Development endpoint modal. Provider options match the Connections editor. Secrets stay ciphertext; they are never returned from APIs.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Click <strong class="text-foreground">Apply resolutions and import</strong>.
                Success toasts how many pipelines landed. Unresolved stubs block materialization
                and keep you on the wizard. Creating stubs is an operator action — Developers
                do not get this button.
            </p>

            <h2>2. Review the draft DAG</h2>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Developer</strong> (or anyone with CanDesign).
                Open the pipeline → DAG designer. Confirm roots (badged; no incoming edges),
                handler types, and edges. Scenario C warns on publish when root count is
                greater than one — you must confirm. Saves debounce; Ctrl+S also saves.
                Cycle detection refuses a bad graph.
            </p>
            <p>
                SQL stays on the draft until publish. <strong class="text-foreground">Open in SQL Editor</strong>
                is Development-only for Developers. Do not paste secrets into step SQL.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Publish</h2>
            <p>
                Designer → <strong class="text-foreground">Publish</strong>. This freezes an
                immutable version: SQL, edges, layouts, retry defaults, failure policy.
                You never deploy a live draft. Further edits go to a new draft. A published
                version opens read-only in the designer.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Create a deployment</h2>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">DevOps.</strong> Deployments →
                <strong class="text-foreground">Create deployment</strong> (modal).
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Name</strong> — e.g. <code class="text-foreground">aw_scenario_a / development</code>.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Pipeline</strong> and <strong class="text-foreground">Version</strong> — the published version you just froze.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Environment</strong> — start with Development. That kind selects which encrypted endpoint each logical connection uses. There is no per-step remap at deploy time.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Time zone</strong> — schedule display; control-plane clocks stay UTC.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Migration policy</strong> — for the lab use <code class="text-foreground">before_run</code> so <code class="text-foreground">aw</code> schema/tables apply on first execution. <code class="text-foreground">on_deploy</code> applies at activate; <code class="text-foreground">manual</code> waits for an operator apply.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Concurrency</strong> — <code class="text-foreground">no_overlap</code> unless you are testing parallel runs.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">DAG failure policy</strong> — inherit the pipeline default, or override <code class="text-foreground">fail_fast</code> / <code class="text-foreground">continue_independent</code> (important for C and D).</li>
                <li>Assignment mode / agent pool — leave automatic unless you are testing pools.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Save, then <strong class="text-foreground">Activate</strong> on the draft
                row (play icon). Drafts do not run.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Trigger a run</h2>
            <p>
                On an <strong class="text-foreground">Active</strong> row, the lightning
                (zap) icon is <strong class="text-foreground">Trigger manual run</strong>.
                Confirm the dialog. The scheduler creates a run that
                <strong class="text-foreground">plans the full published graph</strong>.
                Agents pull leases; the scheduler does not execute SQL. Until DAG/agent
                flags are on, legacy serial execution may still run the same handlers.
            </p>
            <p>
                Scenario F: the Run now modal requires <code class="text-foreground">batch_label</code>
                and the fixture as <code class="text-foreground">parquet.ids</code>. JSON-only
                trigger is 400.
                <a href="/docs/devops-trigger" class="text-link hover:underline">Trigger API</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Optional: calendar-plus opens create-schedule on that deployment (friendly
                cron or advanced). Not required for the first lab pass. Skip this for
                Scenario F — ingest versions cannot be scheduled.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Run history and run details</h2>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Run history</strong> lists runs with status,
                environment, trigger (Manual / Schedule / Promote), start, duration, rows.
                Actions: open details, open <strong class="text-foreground">Run Graph</strong>
                (activity icon). Filter by deployment, kind, status, trigger.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Run details</strong> shows status, trigger,
                timestamps, worker, and step outcomes. From here:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">View run graph</strong> — live visualizer.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Retry</strong> — DevOps, on failed or abandoned. Full rerun or failed-cone (
                <a href="/docs/devops-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">operator retry</a>;
                in-run policy is
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">step retry policies</a>).
                Not on the canvas.</li>
                <li><strong class="text-foreground">Cancel</strong> — pending or running.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>7. Run Graph (visualizer)</h2>
            <p>
                This is the <strong class="text-foreground">published</strong> graph with
                live status (SSE, with polling fallback). It is not the draft designer.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Root nodes are badged. C has several; D, E, and F have one.</li>
                <li>Click a step: Inspector (status, agent, attempt, artifacts, retries, reused-on-cone), Logs, SQL for that published version.</li>
                <li>Palette groups steps and migrations by connection. Migrations apply by deployment policy — they are not extra graph nodes.</li>
                <li>Critical path tab: longest sequential dependency chain.</li>
                <li>Green / running / failed / skipped styling follows step terminals. Independent branches under <code class="text-foreground">continue_independent</code> can succeed while another root fails.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>8. What “done” looks like</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Run status Succeeded (or Failed on a deliberate policy test).</li>
                <li>Destination <code class="text-foreground">aw.*</code> (A–E) or <code class="text-foreground">ingest.ids</code> (F) row counts match the scenario page.</li>
                <li>Re-run is safe: lab SQL is idempotent (<code class="text-foreground">MERGE</code> / upsert). Cheap repeats are author-owned; Querial does not skip “already done” nodes on a normal trigger.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Next: open the scenario page for topology, expected tables, and failure-policy experiments.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</LabsLayout>
